# Browser verification gate

Scope: any change to components, routes, styles, or client behavior. This is a thin gate; the how-to lives in the `verifying-in-browser`, `visual-qa-testing`, `responsive-testing`, and `dark-mode-testing` skills.

## Non-negotiables

- **Never claim a UI/route/behavior change works without running it in a browser and capturing a screenshot.** Reading the diff is not verification.
- Exercise the real interaction (click/type/submit/navigate), not just page load.
- Verify the affected screen in both light and dark mode when styling changed.

## How (short)

- Run `npm run dev` (Vite, port 3000). Note: `dev:all`/`dev:wrangler` are broken on `main` (see `docs/cursor-automation-audit.md`).
- Authenticated views in local dev: use the dev-auth URL documented in `.cursorrules`. Never use production credentials.
- Use the Playwright/browser MCP or built-in browser testing (see `docs/cursor-mcp-cloud-setup.md`).

## Anti-hallucination checks

- Confirm you loaded the route your change affects (right URL, right component).
- Don't infer success from a passing build — build success ≠ correct UI.

## Verification

- Screenshot(s) of the working state (and before/after for visual changes).
- No new console errors in the exercised flow.

## Failure modes / what not to do

- Screenshotting the wrong page or pre-change state.
- Claiming success when a backend-dependent screen only rendered an empty/error state — verify client behavior and document the backend gap instead.
